    "q-34461": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34461",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "How is the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?",
      "worked_solution": "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is equivalent to ${30}^{∘},$ which lies in the first quadrant.\n\nNow, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216822/assets/53bdd11d4204beb60f0b705693ed4d0d75f6a3e26bc3a593b0147852be4baa97.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
