    "q-123891": {
      "dom_id": "step-q123891",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216822/assets/08ee9a92a96f2ab0e4644dc02dfd4be605e72e9b778f54bf60d8e4014d58a7bd.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-3\\sqrt{3},9)$, calculate the angle $\\theta .$",
      "worked_solution": "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is supplementary to $\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216822/assets/e0643cdb2d864b3af6466e24ab815dc8085e811dfdb86cdce253981a521faf82.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{9}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\theta +{\\theta }^{′}=\\pi ,$ then $\\theta =\\pi -{\\theta }^{′}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
